{- | Gate for `E-INDEX-OOB` message parity between `medaka run` and
   `medaka build` + exec over `test/index_oob_fixtures` (#1787, #3187, #3185,
   and #676 part A).

   The prelude's `impl Index (Array a) Int a` / `impl IndexMut …` — and
   String's, and Vector's — guard every `a[i]` and `a[i] := v`. Until #1787
   they raised `indexError` with an interpolated message the native backend
   then threw away: `@mdk_oob` takes no argument, so the same program printed
   `index 7 out of bounds` under `run` and `index out of bounds` from its
   built binary, for years, and nothing noticed. #1787 routes the guards
   through `indexErrorAt`, whose abort formats the index itself; #3187 gave
   `setInPlace`'s own guard the same treatment, in place of a bare `panic`
   carrying no index; #3185 kept List's recursive impl from reporting the
   leftover 0 it decremented to instead of the original index, and taught
   Map/HashMap to name the absent key. Every row here locks that the two
   engines print the same line, down to that number or key.

   `nonexhaustive_guard_chain` is the one row whose code is not
   `E-INDEX-OOB`, and it belongs here precisely because `E-INDEX-OOB` is what
   native wrongly printed for it: a guard chain with no `otherwise` ends in a
   bare fall-through sentinel, which `emitFallthrough` lowered to the same
   argument-less `@mdk_oob`, so a program that indexes nothing aborted as an
   out-of-bounds index (#676 part A). The code is per row, never the gate's.

   Why this is not a doctest: every doctest runs under the interpreter, and
   the interpreter was the engine that was already right. A doctest asserting
   `index 9 out of bounds` passed throughout the years native printed
   something else — which is how the divergence survived. Only a real `build`
   plus exec can see it, and `checkRunBuild` does one per row.
   `diff_compiler_engines` cannot cover it either: its eval arm classifies any
   interpreter `runtime error [E-` as `na`, so these fixtures are ledgered
   `eval:intended-abort` and never compared (`test/ENGINE-DIVERGENCE.md`).

   `index_ok` is load-bearing, not decoration: it pins that every in-bounds
   index at the exact boundaries the guards must not reject — first (0) and
   last (len - 1), read as well as write, Array and String and Vector — still
   returns its value on both engines. Without it, "fixing" this gate by
   making a guard reject everything would read as green. If it alone breaks,
   the environment broke rather than the bug returning.

   Out of scope by choice: the WasmGC arm. `--target wasm` needs a separately
   built emitter this gate's shard does not build. Wasm's side of the same
   parity is `test/wasm/fixtures_modules/w7_array_oob.mdk` under
   `test/wasm/diff_wasm_modules.sh`, which compares stderr and exit code.

   Runs under `medaka test --native`: it spawns subprocesses and reads the
   corpus, neither of which the interpreter binds. -}

import compiler_cli_test_support.{
  RuntimeOutcome(..), fatalOutcomeSweep, rosterAgreement
}
import test_process.{medakaRoot}

corpus : <IO> String
corpus = "\{medakaRoot}/test/index_oob_fixtures"

-- The numeric guards' own wording: the abort formats the offending index.
oob : String -> RuntimeOutcome
oob i = Aborts "E-INDEX-OOB" "index \{i} out of bounds"

-- | The size of the corpus, written down so that a corpus which silently
-- shrank is red. Growing it is expected: add the row and raise this.
declaredFixtureCount : Int
declaredFixtureCount = 15

rows : List (String, RuntimeOutcome)
rows = [
  ("index_oob_read", oob "9"),
  ("index_oob_write", oob "9"),
  ("index_oob_negative", oob "-3"),
  ("index_oob_string", oob "10"),
  ("index_oob_vector", oob "4"),
  ("index_oob_array_set_in_place", oob "9"),
  ("index_oob_vector_set_in_place", oob "4"),
  -- The prose arm: `indexError "…"` straight from source, with no number
  -- anywhere in the line. Its whole assertion is that the caller's own words
  -- survive to both engines.
  ("index_oob_message", Aborts "E-INDEX-OOB" "custom message"),
  ("index_oob_list", oob "5"),
  -- Map/HashMap name the absent key via `debug`: ordinary, empty-string and
  -- whitespace-only keys must each be visibly, correctly quoted.
  ("index_oob_map_key", Aborts "E-INDEX-OOB" "key not found: \"gamma\""),
  ("index_oob_map_key_empty", Aborts "E-INDEX-OOB" "key not found: \"\""),
  ("index_oob_map_key_whitespace", Aborts "E-INDEX-OOB" "key not found: \" \""),
  ("index_oob_hash_map_key", Aborts "E-INDEX-OOB" "key not found: \"gamma\""),
  (
    "nonexhaustive_guard_chain",
    Aborts "E-NONEXHAUSTIVE-MATCH" "non-exhaustive match",
  ),
  ("index_ok", Prints "5\n7\n50\n70\na\nc\n8\n9"),
]

test "every fixture pins a runtime outcome" =
  rosterAgreement corpus declaredFixtureCount rows

test "run and build+exec agree with every fixture's pinned outcome" =
  fatalOutcomeSweep corpus declaredFixtureCount rows
